;;; Two candidates, each refuted in TWO firings — **W1** at layer 0, at
;;; **default flags**. Regression `forced-2-two`
;;; (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two objects; `(q ?x)` taints its object and a taint is a contradiction, so
;;; every `(q ?x)` dies — in two firings, past the one-step lookahead. With two
;;; candidates `alive` is never a singleton, so the forced-positive cascade
;;; (W2) never runs: this is W1 alone.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Root is consistent and every hypothesis dies with it, so root is the one
;;; solution (Q-M1e.6, clause 3): **`Solution`, k = 1, a model with no `q`.**
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered before `fbcae3b2` ─────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   verdict         No solution — the constraints are contradictory
;;;   unsat core (2 facts)   (q one)  (q two)
;;;   exhausted        true
;;;   enterings        2 (dead_post=2)
;;;
;;; Both singletons were entered and died; `phase2` broke on the empty layer
;;; without asking whether root was then complete; `finalise` reported the union
;;; of the hypotheses' cores as the program's.
;;;
;;; ── The control ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `forced-1-two.ein` has the same content with each death ONE firing deep and
;;; answered `Solution` k = 1. Same logical content, opposite verdicts.
;;;
;;; Now both answer R = {∅}, root alone (`ein solve FILE`).
(relation thing T :why "{?1} is a thing")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}")
(relation bad T :why "{?1} is bad")
(thing one) (thing two)
(rule taint () :match (q ?x)   :assert (bad ?x) :why "q holds of {?x}, which taints it: {?x} is bad")
(rule kill  () :match (bad ?x) :assert (false) :why "{?x} is bad, which cannot be")
(solve :no-hypothesis (bad))
(query :goal (thing ?x))
